[38;5;231m⏺[39m Now the heart of the request: what "tasks they care about" actually means. This decides the data model, so it's worth
  getting right before anything else.

  The cheap version is implicit — you're notified about tasks assigned to you, derived from a field the task already
  has, no new tables. The expensive version is explicit subscription — a watchers relation, join rows,
  subscribe/unsubscribe UI, and the question of what auto-subscribes you (commenting? being mentioned? creating it?).
  Explicit is strictly more work but it's the only thing that lets someone follow a task they aren't assigned to, which
  is usually the actual request hiding behind "tasks they care about."

  Adding explicit watching later is a migration plus backfill, not a rewrite — so starting implicit is recoverable, just
  not free.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What makes a task one a user "cares about"? → Implicit + explicit watching (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Next: what counts as a change worth announcing. YAGNI applies hard here — every event type you add is noise that
  trains people to ignore the bell, and notifying on every field edit is the most common way a notifications feature
  dies.

  One flag: comments and @mentions are usually the highest-value notification in a task app, but there's no comments
  feature here either. Picking that adds a fifth sub-project.

